## Conventional Commits

All commits follow the format:

```
<type>(<scope>): <description>
```

### Types

| Type | When |
|---|---|
| `feat` | New feature, endpoint, component, capability |
| `fix` | Bug fix, error correction |
| `refactor` | Code restructuring, no behavior change |
| `chore` | Config, tooling, dependencies, maintenance |
| `docs` | Documentation changes |
| `test` | Test-only changes |
| `perf` | Performance improvement |
| `security` | Security fix or hardening |

### Scopes

Use scopes that reflect the affected area of the project:

| Scope | Area |
|---|---|
<% if (modules.backend) { %>
| `back` | Backend API, services, repositories |
| `db` | Database models, migrations, schemas |
<% } %>
<% if (modules.mobile || modules.web) { %>
| `front` | Frontend UI, components, screens |
<% } %>
<% if (modules.web) { %>
| `web` | Web application |
<% } %>
| `auth` | Authentication, authorization, tokens |
<% if (integrations.cicd || integrations.aws) { %>
| `infra` | Infrastructure, CI/CD, deploy |
<% } %>
| `deps` | Dependency updates |

### Commit Message Rules

- Description in imperative mood ("add feature" not "added feature").
- First line under 72 characters.
- Body (optional) explains **why**, not **what**.
- Reference issue/task IDs when available.

